1. e.getMessage():
     try {
     int a = 10 / 0;
     } catch (Exception e) {
     System.out.println(e.getMessage());
     }
Output: / by zero
✅ Gives only the reason for the exception.
✅ Useful for showing user-friendly error messages.

2. e.printStackTrace()
Prints the complete exception details, including:Exception type ,Error message ,Method names ,Class names ,Line numbers where the exception occurred
    try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
    } catch (Exception e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
    }
Output:java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero
       at Test.main(Test.java:5)
✅ Useful for debugging.
✅ Helps developers find exactly where the error happened.

BEST PRACTICE:
catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
    e.printStackTrace(); // during development
}
In real projects (Spring Boot, Hibernate, Servlets), developers usually use a logging framework instead of printStackTrace():
                    logger.error("Database Error", e);
                    because it stores the full stack trace in log files.


